     {
      "description": "CI passes, a reviewer who is not the author approves the exact head, and the verdict is on the pull request. No queue. Each author runs its own reviewer, so review quality depends on each lane.",
      "label": "The author merges after review"
     },
     {
      "description": "Every merge passes one session's review gates, with one standard. Pull requests wait in its queue, and the queue grows with the number of agents.",
      "label": "PIPE keeps the merge"
     }
    ],
    "question": "Every pull request to binary-equivalence-taiga waits for one session, the task pipeline session (PIPE), to review and merge it. The rule is in the repository's CLAUDE.md, added on 10-24 (#1199) after four pull requests merged with no review. In the 24 hours to 18:46 UTC, 78 merged. At that time, 26 open pull requests had passing CI and were not merged: half were over 6 hours old, 8 over 24 hours. I cannot tell how many of them had reached PIPE. Who should merge? Recommendation: the author, once CI passes and a reviewer who is not the author approves the exact head, because that keeps the review and removes the single queue.",
    "referenced_by_count": 1,
    "state": "open",
    "urgency": "med"
   }
